// Regression fixture pinning the Task 7 review-fix behavior (memory-
// audit-4e whole-branch review, probe P4): a LOCAL container's element read
// escapes to a global. `p4Items` (local `list of text`) is disqualified by
// the existing ExIndex-read rule (reading `p4Items[0]` can hand back an
// element handle the container itself still owns) the moment it's read
// into `p4GSaved`. This probe already passed before the final-review fix
// wave -- it's landed here to PIN that behavior, not to demonstrate a fix.
// The one behavior change from the fix wave: `p4GSaved` (a global) is now
// ALSO tracked by the new whole-program globals escape pass (Critical 1)
// and gets disqualified too (rule 4: assigned from a non-fresh ExIndex
// read) -- previously globals had no aliasing analysis at all, so
// `p4GSaved` was freed unconditionally by `cl_free_globals`, which
// happened to be safe here only by chance (nothing else still referenced
// that handle), not because of any actual check. Expected strict-mode leak
// count: 6 (see ../p4_elem_escape.leaks), three orphaned text objects
// (struct box + data handle, 2 blocks each): `p4Items`'s own list
// container (struct box + backing-array handle, disqualified from its
// local scope-exit free); the one `"a"+"b"` concat handle it holds
// (neither `p4Items` nor `p4GSaved` ever frees it); and `p4GSaved`'s own
// original empty-text default handle, orphaned the instant the index-read
// store overwrites it without freeing the old value first -- same
// orphan-on-overwrite class as P2's `p2G2`, independent of this fix.

var p4GSaved: text

func runP4ElemEscape() {
    var p4Items: list of text
    p4Items.push("a" + "b")
    p4GSaved = p4Items[0]
    alert("p4 done")
}
